# Master Prompt: Native Android Signal Analyzer with LAN Dashboard

## Role and non-negotiable scope

Build a new native Android application that migrates the existing local-first Signal Analyzer. It must run entirely on the Android device (no Python, Termux, Node-RED, laptop backend, or cloud backend for ordinary processing) and must expose a LAN-only browser dashboard served by that *same* Android application. The native UI and browser UI must use the same repository/services/database and must never become separate processing engines.

Do **not** redesign, simplify, or silently change the current parsing, default-risk, duplicate-detection, candle-verification, result-classification, or P&L rules. Port them with behavior-oriented tests before optimizing or refactoring. Do not copy secrets from the legacy SQLite file into source control or logs.

This specification was derived from the working source tree, not just its README. Source references are relative to the legacy project root.

## Existing implementation: facts to preserve

### Runtime and modules

The legacy app is a Flask 3.1.3 application (`requirements.txt`) started by `run.py` on `0.0.0.0:8000`. `app/__init__.py:create_app` creates SQLite at `instance/signals.sqlite3`, initializes it, writes a scheduler boot marker, registers routes, and—outside test mode—starts two daemon threads:

| File | Actual responsibility |
| --- | --- |
| `app/database.py` | SQLite schema/migrations; settings/default risk rules; notification ingest; duplicate/update classification; signal creation and scheduling. |
| `app/parser.py` | The core category-specific parser, normalization, default SL/TP rules, aliases, sizing defaults. 1,519 lines. |
| `app/verification.py` | Deterministic OHLC trade evaluator supporting multiple entry legs and market-now entries. |
| `app/scheduler.py` | Provider selection, Angel instrument resolution, cached incremental candle fetching, verification/P&L persistence, periodic scheduler. |
| `app/providers.py` | HTTP adapters for Twelve Data, Binance USD-M Futures, and Angel One SmartAPI; local RFC 6238 TOTP. |
| `app/sync.py` | Google Apps Script/Sheets polling, payload validation/translation, sync logging. |
| `app/routes.py` | Flask JSON API, CSV/backup endpoints, UI rendering, operational validation. |
| `app/templates/index.html` | One-page dashboard shell with ten navigation views. |
| `app/static/app.js` | Dashboard data loading, actions, parse-review dialog, custom interactive canvas OHLC chart, copy/selection behavior. |
| `app/static/style.css` | Dark local-first dashboard design. |
| `tests/test_app.py` | Legacy Flask/unit smoke tests. Extend its behavioral cases during migration. |

There are no environment-variable reads and no `.env` implementation. Credentials are currently stored as JSON in `integrations.secrets_json`, plaintext in local SQLite; the replacement must improve this using Android Keystore-backed encrypted storage while retaining the same integration configuration semantics.

### Implemented categories and input model

The five seeded categories are `Crypto`, `Forex`, `Indian Options`, `Stock Options`, and `MCX`. Imported notification columns are exactly:

`Received At`, `Notification Time`, `Source App`, `Package`, `Channel`, `Category`, `Title`, `Message`, `Notification ID`, `Delivery ID`.

One raw notification can yield multiple signals. Raw notification ID is no longer unique in `signals`; this specifically supports multi-trade messages. A notification ID is unique in `raw_notifications` when supplied.

### Parser: port behavioral rules exactly

Port `app/parser.py` as a well-tested Kotlin domain module. Keep the named behaviors below (names may change only after parity tests exist).

* `parse_signals(message, category, slRules)` routes `Crypto` to `_parse_crypto_signals`, `Forex`/`FX` to `_parse_forex_signals`, `MCX` to `_parse_mcx_signals`; Indian/Stock Options and unclassified inputs use `_parse_v34_hybrid`.
* It normalizes Unicode superscript/circled digits (`SUP_MAP`), newlines, whitespace/noise, numeric commas, and aliases. Preserve `NOISE_WORDS`, `FALSE_FOREX_WORDS`, `MCX_COMMODITY_ALIASES`, `FOREX_SYMBOL_ALIASES`, and `FOREX_PAIRS` as data-driven Kotlin constants.
* Parse result fields are: category, symbol, direction (`BUY`/`SELL`), `entryPrice`, `additionalEntries`, `stopLoss`, ordered `targets`, `optionType`, `strikePrice`, `expiryDate`, `entryMode` (`limit` or `market_now`), status (`parsed` or `needs_review`), confidence, manual-review state, explanation.
* `_parse_v34_hybrid` detects category, symbols/contracts, CE/PE or CALL/PUT, strikes/expiry, explicit entry, SL and targets. It masks symbols/recognized segments before numeric extraction so option strikes, SL, target and entry values do not contaminate one another. Preserve direction-aware filters and its explanations/confidence, including explicit parsing issues.
* Options recognition includes index underlyings such as NIFTY, BANKNIFTY, FINNIFTY, MIDCPNIFTY, SENSEX; option contracts must retain `option_type`, strike and expiry when extracted.
* Forex aliases include GOLD/XAU → `XAUUSD`, SILVER/XAG → `XAGUSD`, WTI/XTI → `USOIL`, BRENT/XBR → `UKOIL`; supported pair/index tokens are the source constants. `_forex_expand_entry_slice` handles ranges and abbreviated second numbers (for example `3724/20`), and `_forex_parse_target_line` must not mistake TP numbering for a price. BUY targets below the entry range and SELL targets above it are discarded.
* Crypto recognizes slash/attached USDT/USDC/BUSD forms, strips leverage noise, supports `market_now`, ordered targets, and 2–10 independently parsed multi-coin blocks.
* MCX maps aliases such as CRUDE, NATGAS, ALUMINUM and commodity minis to canonical symbols, supports multi-commodity blocks, and leaves option-shaped MCX messages to the hybrid parser.
* Multi-trade splitting only applies where 2–10 headers exist and at least two parsed blocks have a symbol and direction; otherwise parse a single block.
* An otherwise complete `symbol + direction + targets` message can be `market_now`; market price is resolved only from the first available signal-time candle later in processing.
* At ingestion, a non-duplicate Forex follow-up within 30 minutes can inherit the most recent same-channel primary Forex symbol only when the new parse has direction and entry/market-now but no symbol. It becomes parsed with confidence `0.85`; preserve the explanation annotation.

### Default SL/TP and sizing rules

`DEFAULT_SL_SEED` in `app/parser.py` is product behavior, not illustrative data. Persist an equivalent editable rule document. Rule precedence is per-symbol (MCX key is `SYMBOL:OPT` or `SYMBOL:FUT`) then category default. Explicit parsed SL/targets are never overwritten.

* Unit type: Forex = pips; Crypto = percentage of entry; everything else = points.
* Forex pip size: XAUUSD/GOLD = 0.1; JPY pairs, XAGUSD, oils = 0.01; listed indices = 1; other FX = 0.0001.
* Default SL uses the lowest entry for BUY / highest for SELL. Targets use the arithmetic mean of all entries. BUY adds offset, SELL subtracts it; preserve rounding and non-positive-price guards in `apply_default_sl` and `apply_default_targets`.
* Default seed includes Indian index option values (for example NIFTY SL/TP 20/20/40/60 with 65 lot size and BANKNIFTY 40/40/80/120 with 30), MCX instrument profiles, Forex lot sizes, and Crypto USD capital defaults. Port the full literal seed from source—not a shortened approximation.
* Every applied default must be represented in the explanation, including source profile, so dashboard flags remain meaningful. Persist lots, lot size, and crypto dollar capital with the rule.

### Ingestion and duplicate behavior

Implement `Database.ingest` semantics transactionally.

* Reject a second identical non-empty `Notification ID` as `duplicate` before creating rows.
* For same-channel messages within configurable `duplicate_window_minutes` (default 10): equal normalized messages are grouped; earliest is primary and later rows get `is_duplicate=1`, `duplicate_of`, a group ID, and reason `same normalized message from same channel within configured window`.
* A richer message with detail markers (`ENTRY`, `TARGET`, `TP`, `SL`, `LEVERAGE`) and the same normalized subject (symbol + direction) is an update, not a duplicate. Associate a group and mark the newer raw row `duplicate_reason` as `signal_update: richer same-channel message with entries, targets, stop loss, or leverage`.
* Duplicate signals are visible but never automatically verified. Legacy unclassified rows are backfilled non-destructively.
* Parsed, non-duplicate signal rows with a symbol, BUY/SELL, and entry/market-now receive a schedule. Rows older than the initial wait at ingestion are `manual_required`, not auto-queued. Do not retroactively auto-queue historical notifications at app boot.

### Candle verification and P&L: exact semantic contract

Port `app/verification.py:verify_detailed` and `_evaluate_single_leg` directly into pure Kotlin with golden tests.

1. Use ordered 1-minute OHLC candles at/after notification timestamp. A price is touched if `low - tolerance <= price <= high + tolerance`.
2. Treat primary entry and each distinct additional entry as independent legs. Limit entries begin at their first touched candle. `market_now` enters leg 1 at candle 0 open (fallback close).
3. For each leg, scan from its entry candle. Record TP events in target order, and retain the highest target reached. If SL and a *new* TP are touched in the same candle before any earlier TP on that leg, return that leg as `ambiguous` and stop it.
4. An SL only ends a leg when no target has yet been hit on it. A leg stops scanning when all targets are hit.
5. No candle → `awaiting_market_data`; missing direction/entry → `pending`; unhit limit entry → `pending`. Aggregate status precedence is: first TP anywhere → `target_hit`; then any ambiguity → `ambiguous`; then all entered legs SL → `stop_loss_hit`; else `entry_hit`.
6. Persist event timeline, leg summaries, first entry/target/SL times, all-targets-hit, and explanatory text. Do not infer intrabar ordering beyond the stated same-candle ambiguity rule.
7. P&L is sum of triggered legs: Crypto quantity is `usd_amount / entry`; other categories use `lots * effectiveLotSize`; `pointsPnl * quantity`. Crypto and Forex are USD; Indian/MCX are INR. An Angel master lot size overrides category profile only when there is no explicit symbol lot-size override.

### Providers and market routing

Keep provider access behind interfaces/repositories so the verification service only asks for normalized candles.

| Legacy adapter | Migration requirement |
| --- | --- |
| `BinanceFutures` | Public USD-M `/fapi/v1/klines`, pagination to 1,500 candles (1,000 for spot mirror), fallback hosts plus `data-api.binance.vision`; supported intervals `1m,3m,5m,15m,30m,1h,2h,4h,1d`. Preserve retries/backoff and normalized UTC OHLCV. |
| `TwelveData` | `/time_series`; supports `1min,5min,15min,1h,4h,1day`; requires configured key; records returned credit header/estimated credit in `api_request_log`. Converts six-letter FX symbols to slash notation when scheduling. |
| `AngelOneProvider` | SmartAPI login via API key, client ID, password/PIN, RFC 6238 HMAC-SHA1 TOTP; searchScrip and historical candle endpoints; map internal intervals to `ONE_MINUTE` etc. Fetch/store official OpenAPIScripMaster and validate mappings against it. |

`resolve_signal_provider` routes Crypto/USDT to Binance, Forex and recognized FX symbols to Twelve Data, otherwise Angel One. For Angel, lookup mappings first (`SYMBOL|EXPIRY|STRIKE|CE/PE`, then `SYMBOL|STRIKE|CE/PE`, then symbol), auto-resolve active contracts from master/live search, and show a clear missing-contract error. The scheduler may refresh Angel master at most once per 30 minutes when needed.

Important source mismatch: README claims Forex download/verification is disabled, and the manual `/api/candles/fetch` endpoint rejects Forex; however current `scheduler.py` **does route Forex to Twelve Data** and processes it when configured. Implement the current source behavior and retain a user-visible configuration/error state when Twelve Data is unavailable.

Persist deduplicated normalized candles. Existing identity is `(provider, symbol, exchange, interval, candle_timestamp)` (with older compatibility identity `(provider, symbol, timeframe, candle_time)`). Verification requests missing one-minute slices from signal minute through now, using cache coverage to avoid re-downloading existing ranges.

### Scheduling, recovery, and limitations

Legacy scheduler wakes every 10 seconds. It selects at most 25 due rows, atomically claims `scheduled → processing`, runs verification, then either completes/expires or reschedules. Defaults: initial wait 15 minutes, repeat 15 minutes, expiry 168 hours, retry missing candles 15 minutes; all are settings. `stop_loss_hit` and `ambiguous` are terminal. A `target_hit` is terminal only when all targets on all entered legs are hit; otherwise continue. On app boot, pending historical scheduled work older than the current initial wait becomes `manual_required`.

Use a foreground-service-backed scheduler only while user enables “dedicated processing”, with a persistent notification and explicit battery-optimization guidance. Persist the schedule state and a lease/lock before execution; recover stale `processing` leases on startup. Use WorkManager for reboot/startup reconciliation, deferred catch-up and retry, not as a promise of minute-perfect continuous work. Document that Android can stop work under battery/network restrictions. Enforce network constraints and exponential retry for providers while maintaining the legacy retry semantics in state.

## Android target architecture

Build a single Kotlin Android project, minimum API level selected by the implementing agent based on current AndroidX/Ktor support. Recommended components:

* Jetpack Compose native UI, ViewModels, Navigation, Coroutines/Flow.
* Room over SQLite for all non-secret legacy tables, migrations, foreign keys/indexes, and transactional DAO operations.
* Kotlin serialization for JSON columns/events/rules and HTTP payloads.
* OkHttp for provider and Apps Script requests; use Android Keystore + encrypted preferences/database strategy for secrets.
* Ktor embedded CIO/Netty server (whichever is Android-compatible and verified) running in the same foreground service/process, serving static dashboard assets plus versioned JSON APIs and WebSocket/SSE updates.
* Repository/domain layering: parser engine, default-rule engine, ingestion service, market repository, verification engine, scheduling repository, sync worker. Both Compose and HTTP controllers call these same services.

Do not try to execute the existing Python inside the APK. Translate parser and engine logic to Kotlin with tests. Do not make the HTTP dashboard call a second cloud process.

