Existing import strategy: make an explicit, opt-in, rollback-safe “Import legacy database” screen. First copy the supplied `signals.sqlite3` to app-private storage, validate schema/version, then import in a transaction preserving IDs where feasible or create an ID mapping that preserves every FK. Migrate `integrations.config_json`; prompt the user to re-enter secrets unless a deliberate encrypted import path is implemented. Verify row counts, foreign-key integrity, critical table hashes/counts, and preserve original database/backup untouched. SQLite backup files are not automatically imported.

## Interfaces

### Native Android UI

Keep the legacy terminology and workflows, but make them mobile-appropriate. Required destinations:

* Overview: notifications/parsed/today/TP/SL/pending, entry/win rate, INR/USD P&L, category/channel distributions, recent signals and sync/integration health.
* Integrations: Google Apps Script, Binance, Twelve Data, Angel One; credential entry/masking, tests, instrument-master refresh.
* Signal management: category/channel/status/notification-type filters, expansion into raw text/parse/legs/P&L/events, selection, manual edit, reparse, delete and batch verification.
* Safe parse preview that does not write data.
* Channel analytics; market-data contracts/catalog/cache coverage; settings/default SL/TP/size editor; reports/export/backup; system logs; notification log with history filters.
* Individual signal parse-review and chart screens. Render signal OHLC with entry/TP/SL event markers, zoom/pan and crosshair equivalents to the existing custom canvas chart.
* Processing/sync/server status, LAN URL/QR/copy action, access-token/session controls, and foreground-service/battery status.

### Laptop browser dashboard

Preserve the existing dark sidebar dashboard rather than replacing it with a generic admin template. Reuse `app/templates/index.html`, `app/static/style.css`, and `app/static/app.js` conceptually and, where practical, reuse/refactor their CSS/JS as Ktor-served assets. Existing views are Overview, Integrations & API, Signal Management, Signal Analyzer, Channel Analytics, Market Data, Reports & Export, Settings, System Logs, Notification Log.

Retain: category bars/metrics, accordion signal cards, filters, parse-review dialog, batch controls, custom candle chart interactions, market contract selectors, default-rule editor, CSV exports and local backup. Make responsive enough for phone web view, but it must remain first-class on a laptop.

### Embedded HTTP server/API

Serve on a configurable port (default 8080), bind safely to LAN interfaces only when “LAN dashboard” is explicitly enabled, and display `http://DEVICE-LAN-IP:PORT`. Keep native loopback access available. HTTPS is optional only if implemented with a manageable local certificate; never imply public Internet exposure.

Do not expose the legacy Flask API without protection: require a generated per-install bearer token or dashboard pairing flow for all state-changing endpoints, plus session/CSRF protections for browser use; keep the token out of URLs/logs. Default deny non-RFC1918/private source IPs; warn that LAN clients can control/delete/export data. Rate-limit auth failures. Native UI calls services directly, not the LAN API.

Implement versioned `/api/v1/...` equivalents to these legacy endpoints, retaining compatible JSON shapes where dashboard reuse benefits:

* `GET /`, overview, signals/filter/delete/update/chart/parse-detail/reparse/import/parse preview/settings/default-rule endpoints.
* Integration CRUD, sync and sync logs; Binance test; Twelve candles/usage; Angel test/master/catalog/search/mapping/candles; cache coverage/delete.
* Manual individual/batch verify and schedule history; channel analytics, system logs, CSV exports, and backup.

Use WebSocket (or SSE fallback) events—`signal.changed`, `sync.completed`, `schedule.changed`, `market.cache.changed`, `log.appended`, `server.status`—so native and browser screens reflect persisted changes immediately. Events announce IDs/statuses; clients refetch authoritative data. Every mutation is performed by the same transactional services used by Compose.

